<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahaz was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the second half of the 8th century BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekah">Pekah of Israel</a> attacked him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syro-ephraimite-war">Syro-Ephraimite War</a>, he appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> and became an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, a submission recorded in Assyrian inscriptions under his full name, Jehoahaz. The Bible condemns him for introducing foreign cults, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaz.jpg" alt="Ahaz" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahaz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
